# icb_sync_errors: Where the write-back stops (synchronizeIndirect{Render,Compute}CommandBuffer). Each target ICB's layout places one field past its command (or its guest range is shorter than a command), so its own prepare fails and leaves it host-invalid; a blit 305 then copies a well-formed ICB's commands into it, and InvalidateResources (52: set host valid, clear guest valid) makes it need a write-back. The 53 then writes each command until the failing field ("Failed to read command", "Failed to write commandType / pipeline state", "Failed to get attribute strides / kernel, vertex or fragment buffers cursor"): the error is dropped (synchronizeWithCPU ignores it) and the guest copy is marked valid. A compute ICB with dynamic strides sizes its write-back stride cursor by the kernel bind count, as prepare does: one with 31 vertex binds prepares, executes and is written back whole. Keyed by the host (GPU addresses).
